{
  "distractor_3": "Add automation (a pre-commit hook or model generator) that injects workflow tags into new model files based on their folder or chosen template so developers no longer have to tag models manually.",
  "explanation_why_it_is_incorrect_3": "This merely shifts tagging from manual effort to tooling rather than removing the tag surface area. It doesn't fix the 80 existing tagged models or prevent future misclassification when workflows overlap, and it requires maintaining the automation logic as workflows evolve. Auto-tagging can still produce incorrect tags (especially for edge-case models that span multiple workflows), preserves the brittleness of tag-based selection, and adds developer-tooling complexity instead of centralizing selection with YAML selectors.",
  "thinking_process_3": "I looked for a third plausible, commonly used approach that reduces manual tagging without changing selection semantics: automating tag insertion via hooks or templates. That seems attractive because it reduces human error on new files and keeps tags in place for dbt selectors. However, on reflection this doesn't address the root problem (maintenance of tag logic and retrofitting existing models), introduces another moving part to maintain, and can silently misclassify models in overlapping or exceptional cases—so it's a flawed solution in this scenario."
}

{
  "distractor_3": "Extract shared macros into a central ‘macro-only’ dbt project in your monorepo, reference it via a local path in each project's packages.yml, and update the monorepo's dbt config to roll out the new version.",
  "explanation_why_it_is_incorrect_3": "Local path packages only work when every dbt job checks out the same monorepo layout; dbt Cloud jobs that use separate repositories won't be able to resolve a local path dependency. More importantly, the dbt runtime version is controlled by the dbt Cloud environment/job configuration, not by files in the repository — changing repository config or files doesn't automatically change the dbt version used at runtime. This approach therefore fails to scale across many jobs and can break builds unless you restructure all jobs to use the same repo.",
  "thinking_process_3": "I looked for a plausible way teams centralize macros without duplicating files and thought a 'macro-only' project referenced by local path in packages.yml is a common monorepo pattern. Step-by-step: factor macros into their own dbt project in the repo, point each consuming project at that path so they share code, and then attempt to manage dbt version centrally by altering the monorepo config. This seems attractive because it avoids publishing packages, but it relies on every job using the same repo layout and incorrectly assumes repository changes control the dbt runtime — both are edge-case pitfalls in dbt Cloud." 
}
